</history><permissions><!--&#x000a9; 2025 the Alzheimer's Association.--><copyright-statement content-type="article-copyright">&#x000a9; 2025 Eli Lilly &#x00026; Company. <italic toggle="yes">Alzheimer's &#x00026; Dementia: Diagnosis, Assessment &#x00026; Disease Monitoring</italic> published by Wiley Periodicals LLC on behalf of Alzheimer's Association.</copyright-statement><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbynclicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This is an open access article under the terms of the <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/">http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/</ext-link> License, which permits use, distribution and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited and is not used for commercial purposes.</license-p></license></permissions><self-uri content-type="pdf" xlink:href="file:DAD2-17-e70201.pdf"/><abstract><title>Abstract</title><sec id="dad270201-sec-0010"><title>INTRODUCTION</title><p>Plasma biomarkers are minimally invasive tool for identifying Alzheimer's disease pathology. However, evaluation of their clinical utility remains limited.</p></sec><sec id="dad270201-sec-0020"><title>METHODS</title><p>This ongoing, open&#x02010;label, randomized, two&#x02010;arm, multicenter, U.S., prospective, observational study enrolled 609 patients presenting for initial evaluation of cognitive impairment. Patients were randomized into tau phosphorylated at position 217 (p&#x02010;tau217) tested (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;391) and untested (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;218) arms.</p></sec><sec id="dad270201-sec-0030"><title>RESULTS</title><p>Change in working diagnosis was observed for 70.5% of patients with a t&#x02010;tau217 result (positive or negative) inconsistent with baseline working diagnosis compared to 2.3% with a result consistent with baseline working diagnosis and 15.6% of untested patients. When the result was consistent with baseline working diagnosis, a significant 15.7% increase in diagnostic confidence was observed compared to 1.7% in untested patients and 5.0% when the result was inconsistent with baseline working diagnosis.</p></sec><sec id="dad270201-sec-0040"><title>DISCUSSION</title><p>P&#x02010;tau217 testing changed health care providers&#x02019; intended management and working diagnosis and increased confidence in the diagnosis.</p></sec><sec id="dad270201-sec-0050"><title>Highlights</title><p>
<list list-type="bullet" id="dad270201-list-0001"><list-item><p>Evaluation of the clinical utility of plasma tau phosphorylated at position 217 (p&#x02010;tau217) for identifying Alzheimer's disease pathology has been limited.</p></list-item><list-item><p>In this study, the impact of p&#x02010;tau testing on health care providers&#x02019; diagnostic thinking in patients under evaluation for cognitive impairment was assessed.</p></list-item><list-item><p>When the result was inconsistent with the working diagnosis, a change in the working diagnosis was observed in 70.5% of tested patients.</p></list-item><list-item><p>When the result was consistent, diagnostic confidence increased by 15.7%.</p></list-item><list-item><p>P&#x02010;tau217 testing demonstrated clinical utility by changing the working diagnosis, increasing diagnostic confidence, and altering intended patient management.</p></list-item></list>
</p></sec></abstract><kwd-group kwd-group-type="author-generated"><kwd id="dad270201-kwd-0001">Alzheimer's disease</kwd><kwd id="dad270201-kwd-0002">p&#x02010;tau217</kwd><kwd id="dad270201-kwd-0003">plasma biomarkers</kwd></kwd-group><funding-group><award-group id="funding-0001"><funding-source>
<institution-wrap><institution>Eli Lilly and Company</institution><institution-id institution-id-type="doi">10.13039/100004312</institution-id></institution-wrap>
</funding-source></award-group></funding-group><counts><fig-count count="2"/><table-count count="3"/><page-count count="10"/><word-count count="5844"/></counts><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>source-schema-version-number</meta-name><meta-value>2.0</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>cover-date</meta-name><meta-value>October-December 2025</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>details-of-publishers-convertor</meta-name><meta-value>Converter:WILEY_ML3GV2_TO_JATSPMC version:6.6.6 mode:remove_FC converted:17.11.2025</meta-value></custom-meta></custom-meta-group></article-meta><notes><p content-type="self-citation">
<mixed-citation publication-type="journal" id="dad270201-cite-0001">
<string-name>
<surname>Burnham</surname>
<given-names>SC</given-names>
</string-name>, <string-name>
<surname>Hu</surname>
<given-names>H</given-names>
</string-name>, <string-name>
<surname>Tang</surname>
<given-names>Y</given-names>
</string-name>, et&#x000a0;al. <article-title>P&#x02010;tau217 testing impact on intended management of patients presenting with cognitive impairment: A randomized clinical utility study</article-title>. <source>Alzheimer's Dement</source>. <year>2025</year>;<volume>17</volume>:<elocation-id>e70201</elocation-id>. <pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1002/dad2.70201</pub-id>
</mixed-citation>
</p></notes></front><body><sec id="dad270201-sec-0060"><label>1</label><title>INTRODUCTION</title><p>The clinical diagnosis of Alzheimer's disease (AD) usually involves a combination of clinical history, cognitive assessment, structural imaging, and routine laboratory testing and can differ by geography and clinical setting.<xref rid="dad270201-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>1</sup>
</xref> It is estimated that&#x000a0;&#x0003e;50% of patients with dementia receive no formal diagnosis, and for patients who receive an AD diagnosis, AD pathology test results conflict with the diagnosis in 15% to 30% of cases.<xref rid="dad270201-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>1</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="dad270201-bib-0002" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>2</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="dad270201-bib-0003" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>3</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="dad270201-bib-0004" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>4</sup>
</xref> Positron emission tomography (PET), as well as recent advances in cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) and blood biomarker testing for amyloid and tau potentially allow for early diagnosis and treatment of AD,<xref rid="dad270201-bib-0005" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>5</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="dad270201-bib-0006" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>6</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="dad270201-bib-0007" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>7</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="dad270201-bib-0008" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>8</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="dad270201-bib-0009" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>9</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="dad270201-bib-0010" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>10</sup>
</xref> resulting in the reconceptualization of AD diagnostic criteria and research frameworks.<xref rid="dad270201-bib-0011" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>11</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="dad270201-bib-0012" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>12</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="dad270201-bib-0013" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>13</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>Novel plasma biomarkers, particularly tau phosphorylated at position 217 (p&#x02010;tau217), may provide accessible and cost&#x02010;effective indication of AD pathology. Even with rigorous analytical and clinical validation demonstrating that p&#x02010;tau217 tests are highly concordant with amyloid PET, there is a need to investigate the impact of these p&#x02010;tau217 tests on the routine evaluation of patients for cognitive decline.<xref rid="dad270201-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>14</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="dad270201-bib-0015" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>15</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="dad270201-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>16</sup>
</xref> This study aimed to determine whether test results from the Lilly SP&#x02010;X plasma P&#x02010;tau217 assay, commercially available as CertuitAD and henceforth referred to as the p&#x02010;tau217 test, changed the etiology suspected by a health care provider (HCP; for the purposes of this study, termed &#x0201c;working diagnosis&#x0201d;) as well as intended patient management, including any change in planned diagnostic, pharmacologic, or counseling management. The study also aimed to assess HCP confidence in the p&#x02010;tau217 results.</p></sec><sec id="dad270201-sec-0070"><label>2</label><title>METHODS</title><sec id="dad270201-sec-0080"><label>2.1</label><title>HCP enrollment</title><p>HCPs with experience diagnosing and treating cognitive impairment or dementia and currently evaluating patients undergoing an AD workup were enrolled across 32 sites in the United States. All sites routinely participated in clinical research and had at least one neurologist performing dementia evaluations as part of routine clinical practice. All evaluating HCPs provided consent for participation.</p></sec><sec id="dad270201-sec-0090"><label>2.2</label><title>Patient enrollment</title><p>Male and female patients &#x02265;60 years of age with an initial presentation of cognitive impairment, as defined by the evaluating HCP, and for whom AD was in the differential diagnosis, were enrolled across 32 sites.</p></sec><sec id="dad270201-sec-0100"><label>2.3</label><title>Study design</title><p>This 6&#x02010;month, open&#x02010;label, randomized, two&#x02010;arm, multicenter, U.S.&#x02010;based prospective observational study consisted of a screening visit (Visit 1) and three further study visits (Visits 2&#x02013;4; <xref rid="dad270201-supinfo-0001" ref-type="">eFigure S1</xref>). The current analysis evaluated data collected over the first 4&#x000a0;weeks (Visits 1&#x02013;3). At screening, potentially eligible patients were asked to provide written informed consent before assessment. If eligible, data on patient demographics, pre&#x02010;existing conditions, medical history, and concomitant medications were collected. At Visit 2 (Day 0), patients were randomized (9:5) to either the tested arm (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;391) or the untested arm (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;218), and tested patients underwent blood sample collection for the SP&#x02010;X P&#x02010;tau217 assay. Evaluating HCPs completed Questionnaire A (Supplement <xref rid="dad270201-supinfo-0001" ref-type="">2</xref>) for both tested and untested patients regarding working diagnosis, confidence (on a scale of 0&#x02013;100) in the working diagnosis, and patient management plan, as well as experience with amyloid and tau diagnostic tests. HCPs received education on the interpretation of the plasma test results during orientation (see <xref rid="dad270201-supinfo-0001" ref-type="">Supplement 3 for training materials</xref>). At Visit 3 (4&#x02010;week follow&#x02010;up), HCPs completed Questionnaire B for all patients on their current working diagnosis, confidence, and patient management plan. Questionnaire B included the same questions as Questions 1&#x02013;5 from Questionnaire A (Supplement <xref rid="dad270201-supinfo-0001" ref-type="">2</xref>). Questionnaire B could only be completed for patients in the tested arm after receipt of p&#x02010;tau217 results, which were received between Questionnaires A and B. HCPs were required to provide definitive answers to each sub&#x02010;question in Questionnaires A and B before being able to submit their responses. A summary of the administration and purpose of Questionnaires A and B is given in supplement (<xref rid="dad270201-supinfo-0001" ref-type="">eTable S1</xref>). Results of any other diagnostic testing could not be reviewed during this period for either arm. No other defined workup or evaluation requirements were given; HCPs were otherwise expected to follow their typical evaluation workflows for patients with cognitive decline.</p><p>This study received institutional review board approval and was conducted in accordance with ethical principles originating from the Declaration of Helsinki and consistent with Good Pharmacoepidemiology Practices and applicable laws.</p></sec><sec id="dad270201-sec-0110"><label>2.4</label><title>SP&#x02010;X P&#x02010;tau217 assay</title><p>The SP&#x02010;X P&#x02010;tau217 assay is a kitted chemiluminescent immunoassay (Quanterix, Billerica, MA, USA) that detects brain&#x02010;derived p&#x02010;tau217 in plasma. For routine clinical use, the assay has been validated for individuals &#x02265;60 years of age presenting with cognitive impairment, with a sensitivity of 91%, specificity of 90%, and indeterminate rate of 18%.<xref rid="dad270201-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>17</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="dad270201-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>18</sup>
</xref> Per routine clinical testing, the results of this study were reported as positive, indeterminate, or negative; the results report included a brief interpretation (eMethods in Supplement <xref rid="dad270201-supinfo-0001" ref-type="">1</xref>).</p></sec><sec id="dad270201-sec-0120"><label>2.5</label><title>Statistical analysis</title><p>Target sample size (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;360 tested, <italic toggle="yes">n</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;200 untested) was determined based on the expected precision of the 95% confidence interval (CI) for the estimated proportion of change in intended patient management between pre&#x02010; and post&#x02010;access to the assay result in the tested arm. Baseline demographics and p&#x02010;tau217 results, stratified by baseline working diagnosis and further stratified by diagnostic confidence, race/ethnicity, sex, and age were tabulated. Statistical analyses were performed using SAS version 9.4 (SAS Institute Inc, Cary, NC, USA).</p><p>The primary objective was to estimate the proportion of patients in the tested arm who underwent a change in intended management between Questionnaire A (Day 0) and Questionnaire B (Week 4, after review of p&#x02010;tau217 results). Change in intended patient management was defined as any change in response to Question 3 (diagnostic management), Question 4 (pharmacologic management), or Question 5 (counseling management, <xref rid="dad270201-supinfo-0001" ref-type="">eTable S2</xref>). The binary composite endpoint was analyzed using the Wilson score method to calculate the 95% CI around the estimated proportions. In addition, the same method was employed to evaluate change in intended diagnostic management (Question 3; third secondary objective) as well as exploratory evaluations for change in pharmacologic management (Question 4) and counseling management (Question 5). All these analyses were replicated as exploratory evaluations within the untested arm, and the difference in proportions of patients with intended management changes between the tested and untested arms was evaluated using the 95% CI constructed using the Newcombe method and Pearson's chi&#x02010;square test.</p><boxed-text position="float" content-type="box"><sec id="dad270201-sec-0130"><title>RESEARCH IN CONTEXT</title><p>
<list list-type="order" id="dad270201-list-0002"><list-item><p>
<bold>Systemic review</bold>: The authors reviewed the literature using traditional sources (e.g., PubMed), meeting abstracts, and presentations. Plasma biomarkers are a minimally invasive tool for identifying Alzheimer's disease (AD) pathology. However, evaluation of their clinical utility is limited.</p></list-item><list-item><p>
<bold>Interpretation</bold>: Tau phosphorylated at position 217 (p&#x02010;tau217) testing changed health care providers&#x02019; intended management and working diagnosis and resulted in increased confidence in the diagnosis, demonstrating the clinical utility of p&#x02010;tau217 for evaluating patients presenting with cognitive impairment.</p></list-item><list-item><p>
<bold>Future directions</bold>: A follow&#x02010;up evaluation of the impact of p&#x02010;tau217 testing on actual patient management will be undertaken 6 months after full study completion. Furthermore, based on the results of this clinical utility study, an evaluation of the economic value of p&#x02010;tau217 testing for U.S. patients with cognitive impairment is planned.</p></list-item></list>
</p></sec></boxed-text><p>HCPs provided a working diagnosis of either &#x0201c;predominant etiology likely to be AD&#x0201d; or &#x0201c;predominant etiology not likely to be AD&#x0201d; for each patient at baseline and at 4&#x02010;week follow&#x02010;up. The first secondary objective was to estimate the proportion of patients who underwent a change in working diagnosis following receipt of a p&#x02010;tau217 result that was not consistent with the baseline working diagnosis (<xref rid="dad270201-supinfo-0001" ref-type="">eTable S2</xref>). Data were analyzed using the Wilson score method to calculate the 95% CI around the estimated proportion. Change in working diagnosis for the untested arm, and the tested arm overall, and where results were consistent with the baseline working diagnosis, was also evaluated as exploratory analyses. Again, the 95% CI (Newcombe method) and Pearson's chi&#x02010;square test were used to assess the difference between the tested and untested arms.</p><p>The second secondary objective was to determine whether certainty in the working diagnosis increased on receipt of p&#x02010;tau217 results in patients with test results that aligned with the baseline working diagnosis. A summary of primary and secondary objectives is provided in the supplement (<xref rid="dad270201-supinfo-0001" ref-type="">eTable S3</xref>). Certainty, on a scale of 0 to 100, was compared before and after receipt of results for patients in the tested arm with a baseline diagnosis consistent with the p&#x02010;tau217 result (<xref rid="dad270201-supinfo-0001" ref-type="">eTable S2</xref>), by comparing responses to Question 2 between Questionnaires A and B. HCPs chose any integer values between 0 and 100 to express their CIs. This was treated as a continuous variable to preserve data granularity and avoid the information loss that occurs with nominal or ordinal variables, allowing for more detailed statistical analyses. Data were analyzed by <italic toggle="yes">t</italic>&#x02010;test for paired samples. Change in diagnostic certainty for the tested and untested arms overall, and for the tested arm where results were inconsistent with the baseline working diagnosis, was also evaluated in exploratory analysis. A two&#x02010;sample <italic toggle="yes">t</italic>&#x02010;test with unequal variance was used to assess the difference between the tested and untested arms.</p><p>In addition, the following were calculated in exploratory analyses: (1) the mean number of management items changed for patients in the tested and untested arms and the difference between the arms using a two&#x02010;sample <italic toggle="yes">t</italic>&#x02010;test with unequal variance; (2) the proportion of patients who had individual management items (sub&#x02010;parts of Questions 3&#x02013;5 in Supplement <xref rid="dad270201-supinfo-0001" ref-type="">2</xref>) added or removed, stratified by arm; and (3) the proportion of patients in the tested arm who had an individual management item (sub&#x02010;parts of Questions 3&#x02013;5 in Supplement <xref rid="dad270201-supinfo-0001" ref-type="">2</xref>) added or removed, stratified by baseline working diagnosis and p&#x02010;tau217 result.</p></sec></sec><sec id="dad270201-sec-0140"><label>3</label><title>RESULTS</title><p>A total of 609 patients were enrolled in the study; however, 65 were excluded from analysis due to protocol deviations, 3 of whom had also withdrawn. A further five patients (0.82%) withdrew from the study, and one patient was lost to follow&#x02010;up (0.16%). Questionnaire A was completed for 100% of patients in the tested arm (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;354) and 99.5% in the untested arm (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;189). Questionnaire B was completed for 99.4% of patients in the tested arm and 97.9% of patients in the untested arm (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;352 and <italic toggle="yes">n</italic>&#x000a0;=&#x000a0;186, respectively; Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="dad270201-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1</xref>).</p><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="dad270201-fig-0001"><label>FIGURE 1</label><caption><p>CONSORT diagram.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="DAD2-17-e70201-g002" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-1"/></fig><sec id="dad270201-sec-0150"><label>3.1</label><title>Patient demographics and baseline characteristics</title><p>Baseline characteristics, including age, sex, ethnicity, and race, were similar across the tested and untested arms (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="dad270201-tbl-0001" ref-type="table">1</xref>). The mean (SD) age of patients was 73.1 (7.2) years, and 59.3% were female. Among patients, 1.7% identified as Asian, 10.0% as Black or African American, 82.7% as White, and 24.3% as Hispanic/Latino. Data on prevalent comorbidities and concomitant medications in patients are reported in the supplement (eTables <xref rid="dad270201-supinfo-0001" ref-type="">S4</xref> and <xref rid="dad270201-supinfo-0001" ref-type="">S5</xref>).</p><table-wrap position="float" id="dad270201-tbl-0001" content-type="TABLE"><label>TABLE 1</label><caption><p>Baseline demographics and characteristics.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="char" char="(" span="1"/><col align="char" char="(" span="1"/><col align="char" char="(" span="1"/><thead><tr style="border-bottom:solid 1px #000000"><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Category</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Tested (<italic toggle="yes">N</italic> = 352)</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Untested (<italic toggle="yes">N</italic> = 186)</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Total (<italic toggle="yes">N</italic> = 538)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
