<sup>a</sup>
</label><p>Missing is the number of subjects without data recorded for the category.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot><permissions><copyright-holder>John Wiley &#x00026; Sons, Ltd.</copyright-holder></permissions></table-wrap></sec><sec id="dad270201-sec-0160"><label>3.2</label><title>P&#x02010;tau217 test results and baseline working diagnosis</title><p>Of the 352 patients in the tested arm, 134 (38.1%) received a positive p&#x02010;tau217 test result, 160 (45.5%) received a negative result, and 58 (16.5%) received an indeterminate result. Of the 260 patients with a baseline working diagnosis of &#x0201c;predominant etiology likely to be AD,&#x0201d; 116 (44.6%) received a positive result and 104 (40.0%) received a negative result. Of the 92 patients with a baseline working diagnosis of &#x0201c;predominant etiology not likely to be AD,&#x0201d; 56 (60.9%) received a negative result and 18 (19.6%) received a positive result. Similar findings were observed after further stratification by ethnicity and race (underrepresented group [URG] vs non&#x02010;URG, with non&#x02010;URG defined as White race and non&#x02010;Hispanic/Latino ethnicity), sex, and age&#x000a0;(&#x0003c;75&#x000a0;or &#x02265;75 years; Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="dad270201-tbl-0002" ref-type="table">2</xref>). Of interest, the prevalence of patients with a p&#x02010;tau217 result consistent with the baseline working diagnosis (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="dad270201-tbl-0002" ref-type="table">2</xref>) was higher when the baseline confidence in the working diagnosis was &#x02265;80%.</p><table-wrap position="float" id="dad270201-tbl-0002" content-type="TABLE"><label>TABLE 2</label><caption><p>P&#x02010;tau217 results by suspected etiology for all patients, and by URG status, sex, and age.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="char" char="(" span="1"/><col align="char" char="(" span="1"/><col align="char" char="(" span="1"/><col align="char" char="(" span="1"/><c