<sup>b</sup>
</xref>
</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="dad270201-tbl3-note-0001"><p>Abbreviations: CI, confidence interval; <italic toggle="yes">N</italic>, number of subjects in full analysis dataset; <italic toggle="yes">n</italic>, number of subjects in the category; SD, standard deviation; WDx, working diagnosis.</p></fn><fn id="dad270201-tbl3-note-0002"><label>
<sup>a</sup>
</label><p>
<italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x02010;Values were calculated using Pearson's chi&#x02010;square test.</p></fn><fn id="dad270201-tbl3-note-0003"><label>
<sup>b</sup>
</label><p>
<italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x02010;Values were calculated using a two&#x02010;sample <italic toggle="yes">t</italic>&#x02010;test with unequal variance.</p></fn><fn id="dad270201-tbl3-note-0004"><label>
<sup>c</sup>
</label><p>CIs were calculated using a <italic toggle="yes">t</italic>&#x02010;test for paired samples, all other CIs were calculated using the Wilson score method.</p></fn><fn id="dad270201-tbl3-note-0005"><label>
<sup>d</sup>
</label><p>% refers to the percentage level of certainty.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot><permissions><copyright-holder>John Wiley &#x00026; Sons, Ltd.</copyright-holder></permissions></table-wrap><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="dad270201-fig-0002"><label>FIGURE 2</label><caption><p>Percentage of patients with management items added and removed in Questionnaire B for tested and untested arms, completed at Week 4 follow&#x02010;up after receipt of p&#x02010;tau217 test results for the tested arm. AChEIs, acetylcholinesterase inhibitors; AD, Alzheimer's disease; <italic toggle="yes">APOE</italic>, apolipoprotein E; Cog, cognitive; CSF, cerebrospinal fluid; CT, computed tomography; DaT scan, dopamine transporter scan; DMT, disease&#x02010;modifying therapy; Dx, diagnosis; FDG, fluorodeoxyglucose; LTC, long&#x02010;term care; MRI, magnetic resonance imaging; Neuropsych, neuropsychology; p&#x02010;tau217, tau phosphorylated at position 217; PET, positron emission tomography; Psych eval, psychiatric evaluation.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="DAD2-17-e70201-g001" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-3"/></fig><p>When the result was consistent with the baseline working diagnosis, HCP diagnostic confidence increased by 15.7% following receipt of p&#x02010;tau217 results (<italic toggle="yes">p&#x000a0;</italic>&#x0003c;&#x000a0;0.0001 c.f. 0), compared to a 1.7% increase for HCPs in the untested arm (<italic toggle="yes">p&#x000a0;</italic>=&#x000a0;0.15 c.f. 0) and a 5.0% increase when the result was inconsistent with the baseline (<italic toggle="yes">p&#x000a0;</italic>=&#x000a0;0.06 c.f. 0; Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="dad270201-tbl-0003" ref-type="table">3</xref>).</p></sec></sec><sec id="dad270201-sec-0180"><label>4</label><title>DISCUSSION</title><p>This prospective observational study aimed to determine the impact of p&#x02010;tau217 results on an HCP's likely etiological diagnosis, confidence in that diagnosis, and intended diagnostic, pharmacologic, and counseling&#x02010;related patient management in patients undergoing evaluation for cognitive decline for which AD was included in the differential diagnosis.</p><p>Baseline demographics were consistent between the tested and untested arm of this racially and ethnically representative sample from the United States. P&#x02010;tau217 results alone, without review of other testing during the 4&#x02010;week window between questionnaires, had a significant impact on HCPs&#x02019; intended patient management and diagnostic confidence. Change in intended patient management was reported in 85.8% of the tested arm, which was significantly higher than that of the untested arm (75.3%). The mean number of changed management items between Questionnaires A and B was significantly higher in the tested arm than in the untested arm (3.11&#x000a0;vs 2.13). Diagnostic confidence increased after review of p&#x02010;tau217 results, particularly when the p&#x02010;tau217 result was consistent with the working diagnosis at baseline. When the test result was inconsistent with the working diagnosis at baseline, HCPs re&#x02010;evaluated their baseline opinion of the predominant etiology being AD for the majority (70.5%) of patients.</p><p>The high frequency of change in intended patient management in the untested arm may be attributable to the sensitivity of the questionnaire, where a change in any one of the 26 management items would count. In addition, as patients were undergoing initial evaluation for cognitive complaints, HCPs may still have been considering their management plans 4&#x000a0;weeks after the initial consultation. Of interest, one of the management items most commonly changed in the untested arm was the addition of amyloid or tau blood testing, indicating that HCPs were interested in applying plasma biomarker testing across all their patients.</p><p>Management items were changed across all subcategories (diagnostic, pharmacologic, and counseling) for both the tested and untested arms, although with higher frequency in the tested arm where result&#x02010;driven differences were evident (<xref rid="dad270201-supinfo-0001" ref-type="">eFigures S2&#x02013;S4</xref>). Patients with a positive or indeterminate p&#x02010;tau217 result whose HCP answered at baseline that the predominant etiology was not likely to be AD, were more likely to have amyloid or tau blood testing and amyloid PET added to their management plan, indicating a need to confirm the diagnosis. However, they were also more likely to have an AD trial, estate planning, and advocacy items added to their management plan, indicating a level of confidence for changing management based on the p&#x02010;tau217 test. Those with a negative p&#x02010;tau217 result inconsistent with their baseline working diagnosis were more likely to have diagnostic items such as amyloid PET, amyloid/tau blood testing, and apolipoprotein E testing; pharmacologic items such as consideration for an AD trial; and counseling items such as AD advocacy and estate planning removed from their management plan, indicating a willingness among HCPs to accept the negative p&#x02010;tau217 result. These result&#x02010;guided nuances demonstrate that p&#x02010;tau217 testing could make management actions more targeted and efficient, providing more tailored patient management.</p><p>In our study, those in the URG were less likely to receive a positive SP&#x02010;X P&#x02010;tau217 result. Recent studies have indicated that both amyloid beta (A&#x003b2;)42/40 and p&#x02010;tau217 trial eligibility rates differ across racial and ethnic groups, although PET eligibility is similar in plasma&#x02010;eligible participants. These observations suggest potential differences in incidence of elevated p&#x02010;tau217 based on race/ethnicity, but similar correlation of p&#x02010;tau217 with amyloid pathology<xref rid="dad270201-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>19</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="dad270201-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>20</sup>
</xref> However, other studies have had mixed results.<xref rid="dad270201-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>21</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="dad270201-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>22</sup>
</xref> These discrepancies highlight the need for further study of race and ethnicity differences.</p><p>This study had several limitations. Although the HCPs routinely performed evaluations for AD, they operated largely within practices that often participate in clinical research; these HCPs might be expected to be more educated about or interested in p&#x02010;tau217 testing. In addition, HCPs from practices that regularly participate in clinical trials might have an increased percentage of patients for whom evaluation was intended for trial inclusion, as reflected in the responses for intended trial management (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="dad270201-fig-0002" ref-type="fig">2</xref> and <xref rid="dad270201-supinfo-0001" ref-type="">eFigure S3</xref>). Results of studies that include HCPs from non&#x02010;research settings would be more generalizable and informative of &#x0201c;real&#x02010;world&#x0201d; practice, but by nature these studies are logistically difficult to implement. There are also inherent limitations in obtaining study data via questionnaires due to the potential for recall bias, acquiescence bias, bias introduced by serial questioning, anchoring, and order effect; however, comparison to the randomized untested arm helped to mitigate these concerns. Furthermore, the potential for ordering/anchoring effects should be minimized by the &#x0201c;tick box&#x0201d; formatting of the questionnaire, which required respondents to provide definitive answers to each sub&#x02010;question. It is important to note that the surveys used in this analysis were developed internally, are specific to this study, and have not been otherwise validated or applied to other studies.</p><p>This is the largest study to date to examine the clinical utility of plasma biomarker testing for patients undergoing evaluation for AD. The study used a commercially available, clinically validated diagnostic tool that HCPs may employ in routine practice, and it included a randomized control arm to measure changes in HCP intended management in the absence of testing. In addition, the recruited patient population was racially and ethnically diverse, representative of the U.S. population; hence, the study adds to the currently lacking literature on biomarker studies in URGs.</p><p>Plasma biomarkers, particularly &#x02010;tau217, have demonstrated robustness and high levels of clinical performance for the detection of amyloid pathology in a number of studies.<xref rid="dad270201-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>23</sup>
</xref> When considering implementation of p&#x02010;tau217 testing, clinicians should evaluate the intended use population as well as the performance characteristics of the specific test. Guidelines for the use of testing in practice have been developed, including recommendations on test performance and suggested algorithms for triaging and confirmatory implementation pathways.<xref rid="dad270201-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>24</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="dad270201-bib-0025" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>25</sup>
</xref> Recommendations on assay performance for confirmatory test use include sensitivity and specificity, each greater than 90%, which with current assays is typically achieved with a two&#x02010;cutoff approach, resulting in an &#x0201c;indeterminate&#x0201d; group of patients. Recommended management of this group is based on clinical suspicion; confirmatory testing with amyloid PET or CSF sampling is logical when there is high clinical suspicion for AD, whereas repeat longitudinal testing could be considered for those without high suspicion. Of interest, in this study, the intended management of patients with indeterminate results more often mirrored that of patients with positive as opposed to negative results (<xref rid="dad270201-supinfo-0001" ref-type="">eFigures S2&#x02013;S4</xref>).</p><p>A recent study by Monane et al<xref rid="dad270201-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>26</sup>
</xref> investigating the clinical utility of PrecivityAD2, another commercially available test that includes p&#x02010;tau217, in a similar representative patient population, reported that testing resulted in an overall increase in diagnostic certainty, and reductions in amyloid testing and AD medication orders for patients with a negative P&#x02010;tau217 result, and an increase in AD medication orders for patients with a positive result. These findings align with the results presented here.</p><p>This study highlights how a blood&#x02010;based biomarker for amyloid pathology can cause significant changes in the real&#x02010;world management of patients experiencing cognitive decline. The lower cost and risk with use of p&#x02010;tau217 testing compared to amyloid PET scans make it more widely available to HCPs and patients. Even for patients who do not qualify for disease&#x02010;modifying therapy, p&#x02010;tau217 testing can provide patients and caregivers with increased confidence during the diagnostic workup, and has potential to replace alternative, more expensive testing while still alleviating both monetary costs and concerns about the accuracy of a diagnosis.</p><p>Overall, data from this study indicate a clinically meaningful effect of p&#x02010;tau217 testing on the working diagnosis, diagnostic certainty, and intended patient management, resulting in a directed management plan that may improve efficiency while reducing unnecessary testing, burden, and cost to patients.</p></sec><sec id="dad270201-sec-0200"><title>CONSENT STATEMENT</title><p>All human subjects provided written informed consent for their participation in this study.</p></sec><sec sec-type="COI-statement" id="dad270201-sec-0210"><title>CONFLICT OF INTEREST STATEMENT</title><p>
<bold>Samantha C. Burnham, Haoyan Hu, Yifeng Tang, Anthony Sireci, Michael J. Pontecorvo</bold>, and <bold>Rose C. Beck</bold> are employees and shareholders of Eli Lilly and Company. <bold>Paul E Schulz</bold> has received grants or contracts from Eli Lilly and Company, Acadia, Novo Nordisk, Bio, Inc. PSAGE, Eisai, Alzheon, UCB Biopharma, Alnylam Pharma, WBI&#x02013;Stem Cell, Foundation Funded Trials, MD Anderson Cancer Center (MDACC), Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT)&#x02010;Choline, The American College of Radiology and National Institutes of Health (NIH) &#x02013;funded projects. He has received consulting fees from Eli Lilly and Company and Acadia, and has served on speaker bureaus for Eisai, Acadia, and Eli Lilly and Company. He has provided expert testimony for capacity and the effects of traumatic brain injury. He has also served as the Chair of the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM) committee: Stem cell for psychiatric disorder, and has held a leadership or fiduciary role in the Alzheimer's Association and the Frontotemporal Lobar Degeneration Association (FTLDA)&#x000a0;Foundation. <bold>Rosemary D. Laird</bold> has served on an advisory board and received travel support and payments/honoraria from Eli Lilly and company. <bold>Curtis P Schreiber</bold> has received a research grant, payments/honoraria, and consulting fees from Eli Lilly and Company, as well as consulting fees and payments/honoraria from Eisai. Author disclosures are available in the <xref rid="dad270201-supinfo-0001" ref-type="">supporting information</xref>.</p></sec><sec sec-type="supplementary-material"><title>Supporting information</title><supplementary-material id="dad270201-supinfo-0001" position="float" content-type="local-data"/><supplementary-material id="dad270201-supitem-0001" position="float" content-type="local-data"><caption><p>Supporting Information</p></caption><media xlink:href="DAD2-17-e70201-s001.docx"/></supplementary-material><supplementary-material id="dad270201-supitem-0002" position="float" content-type="local-data"><caption><p>Supporting Information</p></caption><media xlink:href="DAD2-17-e70201-s002.docx"/></supplementary-material><supplementary-material id="dad270201-supitem-0003" position="float" content-type="local-data"><caption><p>Supporting Information</p></caption><media xlink:href="DAD2-17-e70201-s003.pdf"/></supplementary-material><supplementary-material id="dad270201-supitem-0004" position="float" content-type="local-data"><caption><p>Supporting Information</p></caption><media xlink:href="DAD2-17-e70201-s005.pdf"/></supplementary-material><supplementary-material id="dad270201-supitem-0005" position="float" content-type="local-data"><caption><p>Supporting Information</p></caption><media xlink:href="DAD2-17-e70201-s004.doc"/></supplementary-material></sec></body><back><ack id="dad270201-sec-0190"><title>ACKNOWLEDGMENTS</title><p>The authors gratefully acknowledge the contributions and dedication of all patients, their families, and their caregivers, who participated in this study, along with trial site investigators, health care providers, and site personnel. We acknowledge contributions from Rebecca C. Anderson, PhD, an employee of Eli Lilly and Company, and Conor J. McVeigh, PhD, an employee of Eli Lilly and Company, for writing and editorial expertise. This study was funded by Eli Lilly and Company. Eli Lilly and Company was responsible for the design and conduct of the study; collection, management, analysis, and interpretation of the data; preparation of the manuscript; and decision to submit the manuscript for publication.</p></ack><sec sec-type="data-availability" id="dad270201-sec-0230"><title>DATA AVAILABILITY STATEMENT</title><p>Eli Lilly and Company provides access to all individual participant data collected during the trial, after anonymization, with the exception of pharmacokinetic or genetic data. Data are available to request 6 months after primary publication acceptance. No expiration date of data requests is currently set once data are made available. Access is provided after a proposal has been approved by an independent review committee identified for this purpose and after receipt of a signed data sharing agreement. Data and documents, including the study protocol, statistical analysis plan, clinical study report, and blank or annotated case report forms, will be provided in a secure data sharing environment. For details on submitting a request, see the instructions provided at <ext-link xlink:href="http://www.vivli.org" ext-link-type="uri" specific-use="software is-supplemented-by">www.vivli.org</ext-link>. <bold>Samantha C. Burnham</bold> had full access to all the data in the study and takes responsibility for the integrity of the data, and the accuracy of the data analysis.</p></sec><ref-list id="dad270201-bibl-0001"><title>REFERENCES</title><ref id="dad270201-bib-0001"><label>1</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal" id="dad270201-cite-0002">
